@meta
id: 27-19
title: The key in front of your keys
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: leader-key, mapping
par: 67
minutes: 8
vscode: a chord prefix in keybindings.json, which nobody configures twice
open: standup.md
vimrc: source config.vim

@brief
Vim has about a dozen free keys and you are going to want fifty mappings.
The way out is a **leader**: one key you press first, turning every other
key into a two-key command of your own. `<leader>` in a mapping stands for
whatever `mapleader` is set to.

This repo's `config.vim` sets one, and defines a mapping on it that marks
a line done. Your colleague's instruction was "leader-d on each of them",
which would be more useful if you knew what leader was here. It is not the
default.

The first three carry-over items are finished — mark them with the team's
mapping. The last three need an owner: define your own leader mapping that
appends ` (peter)` and use it on those.

@keys
| `:let mapleader = ","` | set the leader key — do it before any mapping uses it |
| `:echo mapleader` | what is it now |
| `:nnoremap <leader>o A (peter)<Esc>` | a mapping behind the leader |
| `:nmap` | every normal-mode mapping, leader shown as the real key |
| `:verbose nmap ,d` | what that key does and which file did it |

@hints
You do not have to guess the leader. List the mappings and read the
left-hand column — the team's "done" mapping is in there with its real
key spelled out.
--
`:verbose nmap ,d` confirms both the key and the file. Then define yours
the same way the config did, with `<leader>` in the left-hand side.
--
`3G`, then `,d` `j` three times. Then
`:nnoremap <leader>o A (peter)<Esc>` `Enter`, and `j` `,o` three times.

@solution
    3G  ,dj,dj,d
    :nnoremap <leader>o A (peter)<Esc><Enter>
    j,oj,oj,o  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-eight keystrokes.

The whole of `mapleader` is this: it is a variable, `<leader>` in a
mapping is replaced by its value **at the moment the mapping is defined**,
and that is all. Two consequences, both of which catch people:

Set `mapleader` before the mappings that use it. A `let mapleader` line
half way down a config leaves the mappings above it bound to the old
leader, and nothing warns you.

Changing `mapleader` later does not move existing mappings. They were
resolved when they were made. This is also why `:nmap` lists `,d` and not
`<leader>d` — by the time the mapping exists, the leader is gone.

The common choices are `,` and `<Space>`. Space is popular because it does
nothing useful in normal mode and both thumbs can reach it; comma costs
you the "repeat `f` backwards" key, which you will miss. The default,
backslash, is fine and awkward to reach. Pick one and do not change it —
the value of a leader is entirely in your fingers knowing it.

There is a second one. `<LocalLeader>` is for mappings that only apply to
certain filetypes, defined with `<buffer>` in an ftplugin. Plugin authors
are asked to use it for exactly that, so that a LaTeX plugin's keys do not
collide with yours.

And when a key you have used for months suddenly does something else, the
command is `:verbose nmap <key>`. It names the plugin that took it, in one
line, which is quicker than reading anybody's documentation.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
let mapleader = ","
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
nnoremap <leader>n :nohlsearch<CR>
nnoremap <leader>d A (done)<Esc>

@start standup.md
# Sprint 41 carry-over

- [ ] refund window comparison uses local time
- [ ] gateway schema cache not cleared on deploy
- [ ] payment webhook retries are not idempotent
- [ ] export job times out above 50k rows
- [ ] customer search ignores archived records
- [ ] refund reason missing from the audit log

@expected standup.md
# Sprint 41 carry-over

- [ ] refund window comparison uses local time (done)
- [ ] gateway schema cache not cleared on deploy (done)
- [ ] payment webhook retries are not idempotent (done)
- [ ] export job times out above 50k rows (peter)
- [ ] customer search ignores archived records (peter)
- [ ] refund reason missing from the audit log (peter)
